# Settings that only mean something while something else says so, and what
# happens to them while it does not. Two answers, because there are two kinds
# of dependency here and they are not the same question.
#
# A row waiting on a *switch* is greyed and stays where it is. The setting
# belongs to the feature that switch turns on, so greying it says "turn that
# on to use this" while leaving you able to see that it exists and where it
# lives - which is how you find out the feature has it at all.
#
# A row waiting on a *choice* is taken away. It does not belong to a feature
# you have switched off; it belongs to one of several things the same slot can
# hold, and the others' settings are not yours to be looking at. Three greyed
# temperature marks under a load gauge are three rows about an effect that is
# not on the bar - and unlike a switch, the choice that brings them back is
# the row directly above them, so nothing is hidden from anybody who goes
# looking. See _hides.
#
# An entry is either a key, which has to be switched on, or (key, value),
# which has to be set to exactly that - the temperature settings mean nothing
# unless something is showing the temperature in particular.
# An entry's value may also be several, meaning any one of them will do - a
# desktop scene has a colour to set under three of the nine, and spelling that
# as three separate rules is not something DEPENDS_ON can say. The scenes are
# written out rather than taken from the service because this table is read
# literally by the tests; a value that is not a scene is caught there.
#
# And an entry's *key* may be several, meaning any one of them being set to it
# will do. There are two places to ask for the temperature gauge - the rainbow
# slot in Game Mode, the scene in Desktop Mode - and its marks are in play if
# either does. One rule per place would be two rules that had to be met at
# once, which is the opposite of what is meant; hiding the marks under only
# the first would be hiding the setting a desktop scene was reading from.
TEMPERATURE_SHOWN = (("RAINBOW_SHOWS", "DESKTOP_SCENE"), "temperature")
LOAD_SHOWN = (("RAINBOW_SHOWS", "DESKTOP_SCENE"), "load")
SCENE_HAS_COLOUR = ("DESKTOP_SCENE", ("color", "breath", "patrol"))
SCENE_IS_LIT = ("DESKTOP_SCENE", ("color", "breath", "patrol", "rainbow",
                                  "fire", "aurora", "temperature"))
# Not the temperature gauge, which is one colour standing still: it redraws
# when the sensor moves and there is no cycle for the slider to scale. Not the
# load gauge either, whose brightness is half of what it is saying.
SCENE_MOVES = ("DESKTOP_SCENE", ("breath", "patrol", "rainbow", "fire",
                                 "aurora"))
# The energy preference is only in play under a governor that lets the
# firmware range. Under `performance` the kernel pins it and refuses the file,
# so the menu is taken away rather than offered and then ignored - the value
# is written out as a *choice* rule, which is what gets a row hidden.
#
# "Leave it to SteamOS" is not among them either, and that is the other half
# of the rule: without a governor of ours this project is not managing how the
# CPU runs, and a preference set behind a governor nobody chose is a setting
# whose effect depends on whatever SteamOS happens to default to. Both halves
# are in power.epp_in_play, which is what the applier obeys - so the row being
# hidden and the value not being written are the same decision.
#
# Every governor that does let it through, listed rather than negated, because
# DEPENDS_ON can say "is one of these" and cannot say "is not this". The two
# amd-pstate offers in active mode plus the classic ones a passive machine
# has: a governor missing from here hides a row that should be shown, which is
# the safe way round of getting it wrong.
